Carafe Lab: a flush that asks for no fence goes to d3d12's driver thread without waiting for it. The threaded
context makes such a flush asynchronous only when the driver has a create_fence callback; d3d12 had none, so every
glFlush waited until the driver thread had recorded and submitted all queued work, and Eden flushes after every
guest submission. The callback returns no fence, so a flush that asks for one still waits, as before.
MIT, as the file it changes.

